# .gitignore for cpu2's git-init'd /srv/ (repo root is /srv/, not /srv/docker/
# -- other /srv siblings exist: cron/, data/). Host-specific -- see
# gitignore-db1/gitignore-db2/gitignore-cpu1 for those hosts' separate
# copies; do not merge them, since a finding specific to one host can
# wrongly exclude something another host actually needs tracked, or leave
# a no-op pattern for something that never existed there. `git init` runs
# from /srv/ itself, not /srv/docker/.
#
# ---- scope: only docker/ is tracked, everything else at /srv root is ignored ----
# cron/ and data/ (and anything else that shows up at the /srv root later)
# are out of scope for this repo entirely -- not audited, not reviewed for
# secrets, just excluded outright. Revisit deliberately if this repo's
# scope is ever meant to grow beyond docker/.
/*
!/docker/
!/.gitignore
!/.githooks/

# ---- everything below only ever applies inside docker/, since that's the only
#      top-level entry not excluded above ----
#
# This file holds only host-wide GENERIC rules (secret file patterns, runtime
# data, stale directories, nested repos). It deliberately does NOT list any
# specific per-service file -- those live in a per-directory .gitignore
# instead, one level down, right next to the file it excludes.
#
# sanitize_service_files.py --write generates each service's .gitignore for
# you automatically (only for services where it actually found something to
# redact) -- review its output and copy it to
# /srv/docker/<service>/.gitignore. This root file only needs manual edits
# for genuinely host-wide patterns or genuinely root-level (/docker/<file>)
# loose files.

# ---- secrets: never track ----
.env
.env.*
# .env.example is the deliberate exception: it's the git-trackable companion
# to .env (secrets blanked, config values kept as real values -- built by
# build_env_example.py from a service's real .env). ".env.*" above would
# otherwise silently exclude it too, since it also starts with ".env." --
# this exact gap was found and fixed on db1/db2, applying the fix here from
# the start rather than waiting to rediscover it.
!.env.example
*.env
*.env_*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
*.der
htpasswd
# cpu2's proxy/htpasswd is a DIRECTORY (one file per cpu2 environment, same
# shape as cpu1), not a single file like db1/db2 -- the bare "htpasswd"
# pattern above only matches the file shape; this one covers the directory
# shape too.
htpasswd/
users.acl
ldap.toml

# cpu2-specific: proxy/CA.key is the actual CA private key that signs every
# other cert on this host -- already covered by the generic *.key pattern
# above, called out explicitly here because of how sensitive it is (same
# finding as cpu1's proxy/CA.key). Confirm it's genuinely excluded before
# the first commit, don't just assume the generic pattern caught it.

# NOTE: .gitignore does NOT support trailing inline comments -- a "#"
#      after a pattern on the same line is parsed as part of the pattern
#      itself (making it match nothing), not stripped as a comment. Comments
#      must be on their own line, before the pattern.

# ---- runtime data / logs / backups: not config, don't track ----
*.log
*.rdb
data/
restored-data/
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2
*.tar.gz.base64

# ---- editor/OS artifacts: never intentional, safe to exclude anywhere ----
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# ---- large/misc runtime data directories found by direct review ----
# conda/redis-data/ holds a live dump.rdb (already caught by *.rdb above,
# excluding the whole directory too since more could land there later).
redis-data/

# ---- nested repos: managed by their own .git, not this one ----
# dockprom/ has its own .git (a customized clone of the upstream project,
# same as on db1/db2/cpu1) -- excluded wholesale so the top-level repo
# doesn't treat it as an embedded repo/gitlink. Commit its local changes
# inside the directory itself: `cd docker/dockprom && git add/commit`.
dockprom/

# ---- conda/ is a dev/testing sandbox with heavy, messy clutter (multiple
#      .env variants, many stray backup/old compose and Dockerfile
#      variants) -- excluded WHOLESALE, same category as vault/vault2/
#      infisical below: needs a deliberate by-hand pass, not blind bulk
#      tracking. Important: being in run_all.sh's SKIP array only stops the
#      SANITIZER from scanning it -- it does NOT stop `git add -A` from
#      staging its raw, never-scanned contents. Learned the hard way: the
#      pre-commit hook's key-name check caught real secret-shaped
#      assignments in conda/docker-compose.yaml and conda/entrypoint.sh on
#      the very first `git add -A` here, because this entry was originally
#      just a comment, not an actual exclusion pattern. ----
conda/

# ---- stale/duplicate/abandoned files worth deleting outright, ignored here as a fallback ----
# watchtower/docker-compose.yaml.bak, conda/docker-compose.bak,
# conda/Dockerfile.bak, conda/entrypoint.sh.bak -- all caught by the
# standard *.bak pattern below.
# NOTE: gitignore patterns are case-sensitive on Linux -- bracket expressions
# below match any case, same lesson learned on db1/db2 (a real "CA.pem.BAK"
# was missed by a bare "*.bak" there).
*.[bB][aA][kK]
*.save
*.old
*.backup
*-old/
*_old/
*test/
*failed/
*.back.yaml
*.new.yaml

# ---- date-suffixed stale duplicate files: a shape not seen on db1/db2/cpu1
#      -- conda/Dockerfile-2026-02-27, conda/docker-compose.yaml-2026-02-27
#      (trailing -YYYY-MM-DD), conda/docker-compose.2026.03.14.yaml (dotted
#      YYYY.MM.DD before the extension). Same "better to rm than carry
#      forever" category as the plain .bak files above, just a different
#      naming convention someone used on this host. ----
*-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]
*.[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].*

# ---- cert/key material directories: never track ----
certs/
certs_old/
pki/
tmp-conf/

# ---- everything else inside docker/ ----
# Any file not covered by a rule above (a clean docker-compose.yaml, nginx.conf,
# healthcheck.py, etc. that had nothing to redact) is tracked as its real,
# unmodified self by default. Per-service .gitignore files (one per service
# directory, generated by sanitize_service_files.py --write when it finds
# something) are what carve out the specific exceptions.
